WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
This cell has copied every chromosome.

2
00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.200
Each daughter must get exactly one copy of each.

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.100
But one chromosome is attached to only one pole.

4
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.900
And the whole cell waits for it.

5
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.120
What is it waiting for?

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.340
Look closer at one copied chromosome.

7
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.820
Two sister chromatids lie side by side, held by cohesin rings.

8
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.140
Each sister carries a kinetochore, the patch where spindle fibres grip.

9
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.920
The two face opposite ways.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.460
Fibres reach the kinetochores at random.

11
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.660
The goal is biorientation, each sister gripped by the opposite pole.

12
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.260
But both sisters can be gripped by the same pole.

13
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.060
That is syntelic, an error.

14
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:52.073
How can a cell tell right from wrong?

15
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:54.393
It can sense force.

16
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:58.953
Think of the pair as a short rope, with cohesin resisting.

17
00:01:00.353 --> 00:01:02.513
Pulled from opposite ends, it goes taut.

18
00:01:03.713 --> 00:01:04.793
That stretch is tension.

19
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:08.083
Now go back to the syntelic pair.

20
00:01:09.403 --> 00:01:11.183
Both sisters are pulled toward the left pole.

21
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:13.483
Is it under tension?

22
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.067
In the rope picture, no.

23
00:01:22.347 --> 00:01:25.387
Same-way pulls drag the pair along without stretching it.

24
00:01:26.607 --> 00:01:29.307
Real pairs can still bear some load from other forces.

25
00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:32.607
So the cell should let go and try again.

26
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:38.267
A kinase called Aurora B sits mostly between the sister kinetochores.

27
00:01:39.647 --> 00:01:43.547
It phosphorylates the proteins that grip the fibres, weakening their grip.

28
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:46.907
Slack grips tend to let go and be remade.

29
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:50.987
Under tension, Aurora B weakens the grip less.

30
00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:54.387
Force also steadies a grip directly.

31
00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:57.047
So taut grips tend to stay.

32
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.600
Here is the whole rule.

33
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.640
Keep what is pulled from both sides.

34
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:04.900
Remake what is not.

35
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.860
And cut only when no kinetochore is left empty.

36
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:13.603
Turning over a wrong grip can leave its kinetochore empty.

37
00:02:15.043 --> 00:02:17.203
Empty means without enough end-on attachment.

38
00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:20.963
An empty kinetochore sends a wait signal.

39
00:02:22.143 --> 00:02:25.703
It holds back the APC, or anaphase-promoting complex.

40
00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:29.193
While even one is empty, the cell waits.

41
00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:35.573
In a stuck rat kangaroo cell, Conly Rieder's team destroyed that one empty

42
00:02:35.573 --> 00:02:36.873
kinetochore with a laser.

43
00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:40.773
About twenty minutes later, anaphase began.

44
00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:44.723
The damaged chromosome never reached the middle.

45
00:02:45.743 --> 00:02:47.703
So the cell was not waiting for alignment.

46
00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:52.703
The wait signal came from in or near that empty kinetochore.

47
00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:57.737
A human cell has 92 kinetochores before anaphase.

48
00:02:58.977 --> 00:03:02.437
91 are attached. One is empty.

49
00:03:03.817 --> 00:03:04.817
Does the cell go?

50
00:03:10.717 --> 00:03:15.697
No. One in 92 is about 1.1 percent.

51
00:03:16.997 --> 00:03:18.717
A majority vote would miss it.

52
00:03:19.697 --> 00:03:23.537
The checkpoint works the other way: any empty kinetochore means wait.

53
00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:28.157
When none is empty, the brake on the APC comes off.

54
00:03:29.357 --> 00:03:31.257
It tags securin for destruction.

55
00:03:32.717 --> 00:03:35.717
Securin had kept an enzyme called separase inactive.

56
00:03:37.157 --> 00:03:38.317
Now separase is free.

57
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.070
Separase is a protease.

58
00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.650
It cuts one subunit of the cohesin ring, and the ring opens.

59
00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.010
In vertebrates, the cut acts mainly at the centromere.

60
00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:53.373
And a cut ring cannot be rejoined.

61
00:03:54.373 --> 00:03:58.133
In a normal cycle, sisters are paired only as DNA is copied.

62
00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:04.207
The sisters part. In anaphase A, each moves toward its pole.

63
00:04:05.507 --> 00:04:08.427
In anaphase B, the poles themselves move apart.

64
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.980
One error slips through.

65
00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.140
Sometimes one kinetochore is gripped from both poles, called merotelic.

66
00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.020
It is attached, so it sends no wait signal.

67
00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.860
The pair is partly under tension, so Aurora B can miss it.

68
00:04:25.783 --> 00:04:28.623
Anaphase can start on time, error and all.

69
00:04:29.863 --> 00:04:33.903
That kinetochore is pulled toward both poles, and its chromatid can lag.

70
00:04:34.966 --> 00:04:40.066
In two human cell lines, most lagging chromosomes rejoined the main nucleus.

71
00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:44.046
A minority ended up in a small separate nucleus.

72
00:04:45.099 --> 00:04:47.479
The checkpoint never reads right or wrong.

73
00:04:48.879 --> 00:04:53.199
Tension sorts the grips: taut ones tend to stay, slack ones are remade.

74
00:04:54.399 --> 00:04:57.099
It just waits until no kinetochore is empty.

75
00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:00.699
Then one cut, and the sisters part for good.
